<div class="tab-content active" id="notes-2-2">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What is Supply?</h2>
    <p><strong>Supply</strong> is the various quantities of a good that firms are willing and able to sell at different possible prices during a particular time period, ceteris paribus.</p>

    <div class="callout remember">
      <div class="callout-title">Law of Supply</div>
      <p>As price increases, quantity supplied increases, ceteris paribus, and vice versa.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Why? As price increases, profit per item rises — there is more incentive to produce. The opportunity cost of <em>not</em> producing also increases. Producers shift resources toward this good and away from related goods.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/supply-curve.svg" alt="An upward-sloping supply curve with price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The supply curve slopes upward: higher price, higher quantity supplied.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>A <strong>movement along</strong> the supply curve occurs when price changes — this changes <em>quantity supplied</em>.</p>
    <p>A <strong>shift of</strong> the supply curve occurs when a non-price factor changes — this changes <em>supply</em>.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/increase-in-price--movement-along-the-supply-curve.svg" alt="A rise in price causing movement up along a fixed supply curve to a higher quantity supplied" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A rise in price causes a movement <em>along</em> the curve (a change in quantity supplied).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Non-price Determinants of Supply</h2>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/shift-in-supply.svg" alt="The whole supply curve shifting from S1 to S2, with a new equilibrium" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A non-price determinant shifts the whole curve (here, an increase in supply from S&#8321; to S&#8322;).</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Factor</th><th>How it shifts supply</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Costs of production</strong></td>
          <td>Costs up → profitability falls → supply decreases (shifts left)</td>
          <td>Oil refinery workers get a pay rise → supply of petrol decreases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Technology / labour productivity</strong></td>
          <td>Better technology → more output from same inputs → supply increases (shifts right)</td>
          <td>New mining equipment makes extraction cheaper → supply of oil increases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Indirect taxes</strong></td>
          <td>Tax on producers → costs rise → supply decreases</td>
          <td>Petrol tax increases → supply of petrol decreases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Subsidies</strong></td>
          <td>Government payment to producers → costs fall → supply increases</td>
          <td>Government subsidises solar panels → supply increases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Price of related goods — competitive supply</strong></td>
          <td>If a related good's price rises, producers switch resources to it, reducing supply of the original good</td>
          <td>Tablet prices rise → producer diverts resources from phones to tablets → supply of phones falls</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Price of related goods — joint supply</strong></td>
          <td>Goods produced together: if supply of one increases, so does the other</td>
          <td>More beef produced → more leather hides available → supply of leather increases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Price expectations</strong></td>
          <td>If producers expect higher future prices, they may withhold supply now</td>
          <td>Producers expect petrol prices to rise → supply today decreases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Number of firms</strong></td>
          <td>More firms → market supply increases</td>
          <td>New competitors enter → market supply rises</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Shocks</strong></td>
          <td>Unexpected events (weather, disasters, conflict) reduce supply</td>
          <td>A huge storm wrecks several oil tankers → supply of oil falls</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout remember">
      <div class="callout-title">Explaining a supply shift — the method</div>
      <p>For any non-price change, explain:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>What the change does to <strong>profitability per item</strong> or <strong>the amount that can be produced</strong> at a given price, and why</li>
        <li>How that affects <strong>willingness and/or ability to sell</strong> at any given price</li>
        <li>The resulting <strong>direction of the supply shift</strong></li>
      </ol>
      <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Example: An increase in regulation makes it more difficult to produce, increasing costs of production and requiring new investment. This decreases profitability. Producers become less willing and able to produce. Supply decreases.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">HL: Why the Supply Curve Slopes Up</h2>

    <h3>Short run vs long run <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h3>
    <p>In the <strong>short run</strong>, at least one factor of production is fixed. In the <strong>long run</strong>, all factors can change. The supply curve represents the short run.</p>

    <h3>Diminishing Marginal Returns <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h3>
    <p><strong>Marginal product</strong> is the additional output produced by one more unit of a variable input, keeping other inputs fixed.</p>
    <p><strong>Diminishing marginal returns</strong> occur when extra units of a variable input add less and less to total output because they are combined with a fixed input.</p>
    <p>Example: a car factory with a fixed floor space. The first robot arm produces a large rise in output. The second adds less. The third adds very little — most tasks are already automated. Each successive arm has lower marginal product.</p>

    <h3>Link to marginal cost and the supply curve <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h3>
    <p>Falling marginal product means each additional input adds less output — so more input is needed to produce each extra unit. More input means higher cost per unit. Falling marginal product leads to rising marginal cost. Rising marginal cost means firms will only supply more if the price also rises — explaining the upward slope of the short-run supply curve.</p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="tab-content" id="practice-2-2">

  <div class="callout exam-tip">
    <div class="callout-title">How to answer these</div>
    <p>All of these concern the market for petrol. For each one, decide first whether it affects <strong>supply or demand</strong>, then give the direction of the shift and name the determinant.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Draw the diagram for each. Then pick at least three and write a full explanation in words, following the method in the notes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Supply or demand? The market for petrol</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Oil refinery workers get large pay rises.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply of petrol falls (shift left).</strong> Wages are a cost of production. Higher costs reduce profitability at any given price, so producers are less willing and able to supply.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. Petrol taxes increase.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply falls (shift left).</strong> An indirect tax acts like a cost on the producer, reducing profitability at every price.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. New mining equipment makes extraction of oil cheaper.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply rises (shift right).</strong> Better technology means more output from the same inputs, raising profitability at every price.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. The price of jet fuel rises sharply.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply of petrol falls (shift left).</strong> Competitive supply: petrol and jet fuel are made from the same crude oil, so refiners divert resources towards the now more profitable jet fuel and produce less petrol.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">5. Oil prices go up.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply of petrol falls (shift left).</strong> Crude oil is the main input, so this is a rise in the cost of production.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">6. A huge storm wrecks several oil tankers.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply falls (shift left).</strong> Physical destruction of distribution capacity reduces the quantity that can be supplied at any price.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">7. Global incomes rise strongly, especially in emerging economies.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand rises (shift right).</strong> Petrol is a normal good, so higher income raises demand.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">8. A breakthrough in battery storage makes renewable energy cheaper and more reliable.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand falls (shift left).</strong> Renewables are a substitute, and they have become cheaper.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">9. A war breaks out in a major oil-exporting country.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply falls (shift left).</strong> Production and export are disrupted.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">10. Consumers expect petrol prices to rise sharply next month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand rises today (shift right).</strong> Expectation of a higher future price brings purchases forward.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">11. Car manufacturers release affordable electric vehicles with longer range.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand falls (shift left).</strong> EVs are a substitute for petrol vehicles, reducing demand for petrol.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">12. A new oil reserve is discovered in the Arctic.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply rises (shift right).</strong> More oil is available to bring to market.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">13. OPEC announces coordinated production cuts.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply falls (shift left).</strong> Producers deliberately reduce the quantity supplied at every price.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Extended questions</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">14. Explain, using a diagram, why an increase in the price of a good leads to an increase in the quantity supplied, but an increase in supply leads to a fall in the selling price. <span class="exercise-marks">[10 marks]</span></div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          The question turns on the difference between a movement and a shift. A rise in the good's <strong>own price</strong> raises profitability per unit, so producers move up along a fixed supply curve: this is a rise in <strong>quantity supplied</strong>, and the price change is the cause. An <strong>increase in supply</strong> is different: a non-price determinant shifts the whole curve right, so at the original price quantity supplied now exceeds quantity demanded. That surplus pushes sellers to lower the price until the market clears at a new, lower equilibrium price and a higher equilibrium quantity. Here the price change is the <strong>consequence</strong>, not the cause. Two diagrams make this clearest: one showing a movement along S, one showing S&#8321; shifting to S&#8322; with D fixed.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">15. Illustrate the impact on the market for a product following a government subsidy placed on that product. <span class="exercise-marks">[10 marks]</span></div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          A subsidy is a payment to producers, which lowers their effective costs of production. Profitability at any given price rises, so producers are more willing and able to supply: supply shifts right (or downward by the amount of the subsidy). At the original price there is now a surplus, so the price falls. The new equilibrium has a <strong>lower price for consumers</strong> and a <strong>higher quantity</strong> traded. Producers receive the lower market price plus the subsidy per unit.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">16. To what extent does a supply curve model the provision of software that consumers pay for, such as Microsoft Office, as opposed to a free service such as Gmail?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          The standard supply curve slopes upward because the marginal cost of producing each extra unit rises. Software breaks this: once written, the marginal cost of one more copy is close to zero, so the supply curve would be close to horizontal over the relevant range. The heavy costs are fixed and sunk, incurred before any unit is sold. That means price is set far more by what consumers are willing to pay, by market power and by licensing strategy than by rising marginal cost. Gmail pushes further still: the consumer price is zero, and the actual market being supplied is advertising and data, not the email service itself, so the demand and supply model has to be applied to a different market to be useful at all.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

